---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { getAiPolicy } from '../../lib/data';

const data = getAiPolicy();
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

function bar(pct: number, max = 100) {
  const w = Math.min(100, (pct / Math.max(max, 1)) * 100);
  return `width:${w.toFixed(1)}%`;
}
---
<Layout title="AI opt-out signals">
  <h1>AI opt-out signals</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Publishers have at least nine independent technical mechanisms for signalling that they do not
    want their images and articles used to train generative-AI models. They sit at different layers
    (site-wide files, HTTP headers, embedded image metadata) and were proposed by different bodies
    over a short period — so adoption is uneven and the signals do not always agree. Article 4 of
    the EU DSM Directive requires opt-outs to be expressed in a <em>machine-readable</em> form;
    this page measures which of the candidate machine-readable forms publishers actually use.
  </p>

  <p class="lede-meta">
    Based on the most recent crawl: <strong>{data.n_sites}</strong> publishers probed,
    <strong>{data.n_images.toLocaleString()}</strong> images analysed.
  </p>

  <h2>Adoption snapshot</h2>
  <div class="signal-grid">
    {data.signals.map(s => (
      <a href={`${base}/ai-policy/${s.key}/`} class={`signal-card scope-${s.scope}`}>
        <div class="scope-pill">{s.scope === 'site' ? 'per site' : 'per image'}</div>
        <div class="pct">{s.pct.toFixed(1)}%</div>
        <div class="label">{s.label}</div>
        <div class="note">{s.note}</div>
        <div class="frac">{s.num.toLocaleString()} of {s.denom.toLocaleString()} →</div>
      </a>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>robots.txt — AI bot block matrix</h2>
  <p>
    For each known AI/scraper user-agent, the share of crawled sites whose <code>robots.txt</code>
    disallows that UA at the root. List is versioned in <code>known_uas.yaml</code>; we welcome
    pull requests adding bots we have missed.
  </p>
  <table class="sortable">
    <thead><tr>
      <th>User-agent</th>
      <th>Operator</th>
      <th class="num">% sites blocking</th>
      <th data-nosort="true">&nbsp;</th>
    </tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {data.ai_bots.map(b => (
        <tr>
          <td><code>{b.ua}</code></td>
          <td>{b.operator}</td>
          <td class="num">{b.pct_blocked.toFixed(1)}%</td>
          <td class="barcell"><span class="bar" style={bar(b.pct_blocked)}></span></td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>How many AI UAs does each site block?</h2>
  <p>
    A site can block zero, one, or many of the {data.ai_bots.length} tracked AI UAs. The distribution
    reveals whether publishers are picking individual bots to refuse or applying a blanket policy.
  </p>
  <table class="sortable">
    <thead><tr>
      <th>UAs blocked</th>
      <th class="num">Sites</th>
      <th class="num">% of sites</th>
      <th data-nosort="true">&nbsp;</th>
    </tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {data.block_buckets.map(b => (
        <tr>
          <td>{b.range}</td>
          <td class="num">{b.count}</td>
          <td class="num">{b.pct.toFixed(1)}%</td>
          <td class="barcell"><span class="bar" style={bar(b.pct, 50)}></span></td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Image-level signals</h2>
  <p>
    Three of the eight mechanisms live <em>inside</em> the image file (or its HTTP response),
    not on a site-wide file. They travel with the image when it is copied or shared.
  </p>
  <ul class="image-signals">
    <li>
      <strong>IPTC PLUS:DataMining</strong> — a controlled-vocabulary value in the XMP-plus
      namespace expressing whether data mining is permitted. We parse this from every image
      we fetch.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>CAWG training-and-data-mining assertion</strong> — a structured assertion inside a
      C2PA manifest declaring training/mining permissions. Spec at <a
      href="https://cawg.io/training-and-data-mining/1.1/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      cawg.io</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>noai / noimageai directives</strong> — informal but in-the-wild; expressed via
      <code>X-Robots-Tag</code> response headers on the image, or <code>&lt;meta name="robots"&gt;</code>
      on the host article page. Originated in DeviantArt's 2022 policy; not standardised.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Do the signals agree?</h2>
  <p>
    A site that takes AI opt-out seriously might use several mechanisms together. In practice the
    overlap is patchy — partly because the signals serve different audiences (robots.txt for
    crawlers, TDMRep for the EU legal framework, IPTC PLUS:DataMining for downstream image
    consumers).
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr>
      <th>If a site…</th>
      <th>…does it also…</th>
      <th class="num">% overlap</th>
      <th class="num">N</th>
    </tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {data.convergence.map(c => (
        <tr>
          <td>{c.a}</td>
          <td>{c.b}</td>
          <td class="num">{c.a_count ? c.pct.toFixed(0) + '%' : '—'}</td>
          <td class="num">{c.a_count}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>What we measure (methodology)</h2>
  <ul class="methodology">
    <li><strong>robots.txt</strong>: fetched once per run, parsed with <code>protego</code>.
      For each tracked UA we record <code>allowed</code> or <code>disallowed</code> at the site
      root.</li>
    <li><strong>tdmrep.json</strong>: HTTP probe of <code>/.well-known/tdmrep.json</code>; recorded
      as present + saved verbatim. Parsing of TDM-policy URLs and the <code>tdm-reservation</code>
      flag is planned.</li>
    <li><strong>ai.txt</strong>: HTTP probe of <code>/ai.txt</code>; presence flag only at first.</li>
    <li><strong>RSL</strong>: scan robots.txt for a <code>License:</code> directive linking to an
      RSL XML file (<a href="https://rslstandard.org/guide/robots-txt" target="_blank"
      rel="noopener">spec</a>). Presence flag and the licence URL; we will not parse the XML at
      first.</li>
    <li><strong>Content-Signal</strong>: scan robots.txt for Cloudflare
      <code>Content-Signal:</code> directives (<a href="https://contentsignals.org/"
      target="_blank" rel="noopener">contentsignals.org</a>). Each line is a
      comma-separated list of <code>&lt;signal&gt;=yes|no</code> pairs where
      <code>&lt;signal&gt;</code> is one of <code>search</code>, <code>ai-input</code>,
      <code>ai-train</code>; merged across lines with last-wins. We record the
      presence flag plus each signal's value.</li>
    <li><strong>trust.txt</strong>: HTTP probe of <code>/.well-known/trust.txt</code> and
      <code>/trust.txt</code>; parse for the <code>datatrainingallowed=no</code> directive.
      Presence flag and the directive value are recorded.</li>
    <li><strong>noai/noimageai</strong>: scanned in HTTP response headers (image and host article)
      and in the host article's <code>&lt;meta name="robots"&gt;</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>IPTC PLUS:DataMining</strong>: already extracted via ExifTool on every image.</li>
    <li><strong>CAWG training-and-data-mining</strong>: parsed from C2PA manifest assertions when
      label matches <code>cawg.training-mining</code> or <code>cawg.training-and-data-mining</code>.</li>
  </ul>
</Layout>

<style>
  .lede-meta { color: var(--iptc-muted, #6b7280); font-size: 0.95rem; margin: -0.5rem 0 1.5rem; }
  .signal-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 1rem 0 2rem;
  }
  .signal-card {
    border: 1px solid var(--iptc-border, #e5e7eb);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 1rem;
    background: white;
    position: relative;
    display: block;
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: transform 80ms ease, box-shadow 80ms ease;
  }
  .signal-card:hover {
    transform: translateY(-1px);
    box-shadow: 0 4px 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.06);
  }
  .signal-card.scope-site { border-left: 4px solid var(--iptc-navy, #234976); }
  .signal-card.scope-image { border-left: 4px solid var(--iptc-green, #6db33f); }
  .scope-pill {
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--iptc-muted, #6b7280);
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }
  .signal-card .pct {
    font-size: 1.9rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--iptc-navy, #234976);
    line-height: 1;
  }
  .signal-card .label {
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
  .signal-card .note {
    color: var(--iptc-muted, #6b7280);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    margin-top: 0.3rem;
  }
  .signal-card .frac {
    color: var(--iptc-muted, #6b7280);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .barcell {
    width: 30%;
    min-width: 100px;
  }
  .bar {
    display: inline-block;
    height: 10px;
    background: var(--iptc-green, #6db33f);
    border-radius: 2px;
  }
  ul.image-signals li, ul.methodology li {
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }
</style>
